  "marks": 3,
  "estimatedSeconds": 270,
  "stem": "By writing $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$, use the quotient rule to show that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\sec x) = \\sec x\\tan x$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 uses $u = 1$, $u' = 0$, $v = \\cos x$, $v' = -\\sin x$ in the quotient rule",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "A1 obtains $\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^{2}x}$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 writes it as $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\sec x\\tan x$",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\right) = \\frac{\\cos x \\cdot 0 - 1 \\cdot (-\\sin x)}{\\cos^{2}x} = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos^{2}x} = \\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\sec x\\tan x\n$$"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The derivative of a constant numerator is 0, which leaves one term.",
  "hints": [
   "The derivative of the numerator 1 is 0."
  ],
  "skill": "APPLICATION",
  "calculator": false
 },
